# Design plan: t6_r1_noceiling — smartphone cell (950 Wh/L, 4C no-plating, ≤50°C, SEI≤500nm, plateau≥4.1V)

## 1. Objective decomposition
- Volumetric ED ≥ 950 Wh/L (contract: ∫V·I_1C dt / Σ(thickness×area); electrolyte/casing excluded) — architecture lever: dead-layer thinning (separator/CC), capacity-limited-electrode (negative) loading ↑ via active fraction or thickness, V̄ via IR reduction.
- 4C fast charge no plating: anode potential never < 0 V vs Li/Li+ at 4C charge from empty at 45°C. Levers: neg particle radius ↓, neg porosity (transport), N/P, thickness. Trade-off vs ED.
- T_max ≤ 50°C (323.15 K) at 4C/45°C: levers: cooling h (10→25), cell design; trade-off vs nothing (free-ish).
- SEI ≤ 500 nm @ 100 cyc 1C: baseline ~449 nm (skill reference) — expect pass; watch dense designs.
- Plateau ≥ 4.1 V: NMC811/graphite midpoint ~3.6 V — material gap (cathode OCP); architecture can move only tens of mV. ceiling_escalation OFF + no named systems → cannot fix within boundary. EXPECTED FAIL; three-strike questioning will document.

## 2. Candidate strategy
- R1 baseline characterization + 3 ED-direction variants.
- R2+ 4C safety on finalists; particle-size/porosity refinements.
- Predicted Pareto: ED ↔ 4C (dense thick cells plate); Tmax via h.

## 3. Budget: ~5-7 rounds; spme screening, dfn for 4C finals, aging on ≤3 candidates.

## 4. Risk & fallback
- Plateau unreachable → three-strike → question boundary (LNMO would give 4.17 V midpoint but is a system switch — forbidden by ablation; document as "if relaxed").
- Plating at 4C → smaller neg particles, higher neg porosity, lower N/P... wait no — careful: plating cure = higher N/P or thinner neg? Plating happens at high charge current on negative: reduce local current density (smaller particles → more area; higher porosity → better transport; thicker neg → more capacity buffer... thicker neg helps plating but volume cost).
- Tmax → h ↑.
- If ED can't reach 950 in architecture space → record ceiling honestly (ceiling assessment documented but no escalation).

